"Internal Error ()\nciteturn2669view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2663view0\",\"id\":5}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://cdn.openai.com/threat-intelligence-reports/7d662b68-952f-4dfd-a2f2-fe55b041cc4a/disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-october-2025.pdf: (400) Content length is too large: 13394397\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGemini Computer Use External Model Card (October 7, 2025) - updated2 (https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/Model-Cards/Gemini-2-5-Computer-Use-Model-Card.pdf)\nciteturn2669view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 5; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2663view1\",\"id\":68}); Total lines: 132\nL0@P0: Model card published/updated OCT 07, 2025\nL1@P0: Gemini 2.5\nL2@P0: Computer Use\nL3@P0-1: Model CardGemini 2.5 Computer Use - Model Card\nL4@P1: Model Cards are intended to provide essential information on Gemini models, including known\nL5@P1: limitations, mitigation approaches, and safety performance. Model cards may be updated from\nL6@P1: time-to-time; for example, to include updated evaluations as the model is improved or revised.\nL7@P1: Technical Reports are similar to academic papers, and describe models’ capabilities, limitations and\nL8@P1: performance benchmarks. The Gemini 2.5 technical report contains additional details about the Gemini\nL9@P1: 2.5 series of models that are generally available. We recommend that readers seeking more details and\nL10@P1: information about these models navigate to the technical report.\nL11@P1: Published: October 7, 2025\nL12@P1: Model Information\nL13@P1: Description: The Gemini 2.5 Computer Use model is based on the Gemini 2.5 Pro (06-2025) model,\nL14@P1: including architecture, training data, implementation and sustainability, with post-training to improve UI\nL15@P1: control capabilities. The model has primarily been optimized for understanding and interacting with\nL16@P1: web browsers. It has not yet been optimized for OS-level control or mobile control, although it\nL17@P1: demonstrates promise for mobile UI control tasks.\nL18@P1: The model’s core capabilities are exposed through a new computer use tool that enables applications\nL19@P1: to interact with and automate tasks in the browser. Using screenshots, the model can \"see\" a computer\nL20@P1: screen, and \"act\" by generating specific UI actions like mouse clicks and keyboard inputs.\nL21@P1: ● Inputs: Text strings (e.g., a question or a prompt), screenshots of the environment state, and a\nL22@P1: history of recent actions\nL23@P1: ● Outputs: Function call of the proposed next action\nL24@P1: Evaluation\nL25@P1: Approach: The Gemini 2.5 Computer Use Model was evaluated using the methodology below:\nL26@P1-2: 2● Online Mind2Web: following an industry standard for evaluating web agents\nL27@P2: ● WebVoyager: following an industry standard for evaluating web agents\nL28@P2: ● Android World: testing generalization to another surface and action space\nL29@P2: Results: The Gemini 2.5 Computer Use model demonstrates strong performance on multiple web and\nL30@P2: mobile control benchmarks. The table below includes results from self-reported numbers, evaluations\nL31@P2: run by Browserbase and evaluations we ran ourselves. Unless otherwise indicated, scores shown are for\nL32@P2: computer use tools exposed via API.\nL33@P2: Benchmark Source / Harness\nL34@P2: Gemini 2.5\nL35@P2: Computer Use\nL36@P2: (10-2025)\nL37@P2: Claude\nL38@P2: Sonnet 4.5\nL39@P2: Claude\nL40@P2: Sonnet 4\nL41@P2: OpenAI\nL42@P2: Computer\nL43@P2: Using Agent\nL44@P2: Model\nL45@P2: Online-Mind2Web Official Leaderboard 69.0% — — 61.3% (Operator)\nL46@P2: Measured by Browserbase 65.7% 55.0% 61.0% 44.3%\nL47@P2: WebVoyager Official Leaderboard 88.9% — — 87.0%\nL48@P2: Measured by Browserbase 79.9% 71.4% 69.4% 61.0%\nL49@P2: Android World Measured by Google DeepMind 69.7% 56.0% 62.1% Could not measure -\nL50@P2: no model access\nL51@P2: OSWorld self-reported\nL52@P2: OS control not yet\nL53@P2: supported\nL54@P2: 61.4% 42.2% 38.1%\nL55@P2: Because computer use evaluations are sensitive to the agent’s environment and system instructions,\nL56@P2: additional details on how we evaluated the Gemini 2.5 Computer Use model, including environments,\nL57@P2: prompts, and methodologies can be found here.\nL58@P2: Intended Use & Limitations\nL59@P2: Benefit and Intended Usage: The Gemini 2.5 Computer Use Model is capable of understanding UI\nL60@P2: screenshots, planning paths through computer systems, and predicting actions required to accomplish\nL61@P2: specified tasks in these systems.\nL62@P2: It is well-suited for the following use cases:\nL63@P2: ● Automating tasks in web browsers (i.e. clicking buttons, selecting menus, inserting text into\nL64@P2: fields)\nL65@P2: ● Engaging in transactions on behalf of the user (i.e. organizing files, making appointments)\nL66@P2-3: 3Known Limitations: The Gemini 2.5 Computer Use Model may exhibit some of the general limitations\nL67@P3: of foundation models, as it is based off of Gemini 2.5 Pro, such as hallucinations, and limitations around\nL68@P3: causal understanding, complex logical deduction, and counterfactual reasoning. The knowledge cutoff\nL69@P3: date was January 2025. See the Ethics and Safety section below for additional information on known\nL70@P3: limitations.\nL71@P3: Ethics and Safety\nL72@P3: Evaluation Approach: As the Gemini 2.5 Computer Use Model is based off of Gemini 2.5 Pro, we rely\nL73@P3: on Ethics & Safety evaluations reported for Gemini 2.5 Pro. Additionally, this model was developed in\nL74@P3: partnership with internal Google safety, security, and responsibility teams. A range of evaluations and\nL75@P3: red teaming activities were conducted to help improve the model and inform decision-making. These\nL76@P3: evaluations and activities align with Google's AI Principles and responsible AI approach, as well as\nL77@P3: Google's Generative AI policies (e.g. Gen AI Prohibited Use Policy and the Gemini API Additional Terms\nL78@P3: of Service).\nL79@P3: Evaluation types included, but were not limited to:\nL80@P3: ● Training/Development Evaluations including automated and human evaluations carried out\nL81@P3: continuously throughout and after the model’s training, to monitor its progress and\nL82@P3: performance\nL83@P3: ● Human Red Teaming conducted by specialist teams across the policies and desiderata,\nL84@P3: deliberately trying to spot weaknesses and ensure the model adheres to safety policies and\nL85@P3: desired outcomes\nL86@P3: ● Assurance Evaluations conducted by human evaluators independent of the model\nL87@P3: development team, and assess responsibility and safety governance decisions\nL88@P3: ● Ethics & Safety Reviews were conducted ahead of the model’s release\nL89@P3: Training and Development Evaluation Results: The Gemini 2.5 Computer Use Model is based onoff\nL90@P3: of Gemini 2.5 Pro, and results and their respective risks and mitigations for some of the internal safety\nL91@P3: evaluations conducted during the development phase are listed below.\nL92@P3: We have additionally focused on addressing three risk categories and known safety limitations around\nL93@P3: (i) intentional adversarial misuse (ii) unintentional model failure modes, and (iii) release of sensitive or\nL94@P3: harmful information. See Additional Risks & Mitigations.\nL95@P3: Additional Risks & Mitigations: Details of the three risk categories are below.\nL96@P3: ● Intentional adversarial misuse of the model, for example through prompt injection tactics,\nL97@P3: where adversarial instructions are embedded in websites. This includes scenarios where the\nL98@P3: model encounters web content in which it is difficult to evaluate the reliability of information\nL99@P3: and sources. As the model tries to achieve the user’s goal, it may rely on these less reliable\nL100@P3: sources of information and instructions from the screen.\nL101@P3-4: 4● Unintentional model failure modes through benign use, e.g. where the model takes an\nL102@P4: irreversible action that could harm a user. The model can misinterpret a user's goal or webpage\nL103@P4: content, causing it to take incorrect actions like clicking the wrong button or filling the wrong\nL104@P4: form. This can lead to failed tasks or data exfiltration.\nL105@P4: ● Release of sensitive or harmful information by generating or otherwise outputting hateful,\nL106@P4: biased, discriminatory, illegal, or sensitive/personal content.\nL107@P4: Mitigations: Safety and responsibility were built into the Computer Use model through pre-training,\nL108@P4: post-training, inference-time interventions, and policy prohibitions; specifically focused on\nL109@P4: product-level mitigations such as the following:\nL110@P4: ● Post-training mitigations included training the model to recognize when it is tasked with a\nL111@P4: high-stakes action (e.g. making purchases, downloading files, or sending communications on\nL112@P4: behalf of a user). The model is trained to follow System Instructions (added by a developer) to\nL113@P4: request user confirmation before taking high-stakes actions.\nL114@P4: ● Inference-time mitigations included implementing a safety monitoring and filtering system\nL115@P4: that observes model actions and (a) prevents the model from taking high-risk actions that are\nL116@P4: prohibited by Google policies, (b) requires user confirmations before taking high-stakes\nL117@P4: actions, and (c) blocks access to sites known to have illegal or dangerous content. More\nL118@P4: specifically,\nL119@P4: ○ User-confirmations: Actions that pose a higher level of risk, such as completing\nL120@P4: transactions like financial or retail payments; accessing sensitive information like health\nL121@P4: or financial records; sending communications; or, modifying/managing files, require\nL122@P4: careful consideration and human confirmation from a developer or user before\nL123@P4: proceeding The applicable terms prohibit developers from bypassing human\nL124@P4: confirmation, where it is required.\nL125@P4: ○ Guardrails to screen inputs and outputs: The API uses Gemini’s LLM, with system\nL126@P4: Instructions, to guide the model's behavior and safety guidelines and benefits from\nL127@P4: Gemini’s content filters to block harmful outputs.\nL128@P4: Frontier Safety Assessment: Because model usage is restricted to the Gemini 2.5 Computer Use tool,\nL129@P4: the scope of capabilities is limited to browser and mobile user interface controls; it is therefore not in\nL130@P4: scope for a Frontier Safety Framework assessment.\nL131@P4: 5\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGemini Computer Use External Model Card - Additional Information - update2 (https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/gemini/computer_use_eval_additional_info.pdf)\nciteturn2669view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 5; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2663view1\",\"id\":64}); Total lines: 129\nL0@P0: Published/updated OCT 07, 2025\nL1@P0: Gemini 2.5\nL2@P0: Computer Use\nL3@P0: Additional\nL4@P0-1: InformationGoogle DeepMind – Gemini 2.5 Computer Use\nL5@P1: Because computer use evals are sensitive to the agent’s environment and system instructions, this\nL6@P1: document provides details on how we evaluated the Gemini 2.5 Computer Use model, including\nL7@P1: environments, prompts, and methodologies.\nL8@P1: Published: October 7, 2025\nL9@P1: Environment\nL10@P1: We evaluated our model in three distinct settings:\nL11@P1: 1. Online-Mind2Web, following an industry standard for evaluating web agents\nL12@P1: 2. WebVoyager, following an industry standard for evaluating web agents\nL13@P1: 3. AndroidWorld, testing generalization to another surface and action space\nL14@P1: Some evaluations were conducted by Browserbase on its harness, which allows for normalizing for\nL15@P1: variations across APIs and eval prompt sets.\nL16@P1: Prompting & Methodologies\nL17@P1: Online-Mind2Web\nL18@P1: For Online-Mind2Web, one of the requirements is to use the specified starting website, and not to use a\nL19@P1: search engine or a navigate tool to switch directly to an alternative site, which are two of the Supported\nL20@P1: UI Actions. To abide by this, we set the excluded functions parameter in the open source reference\nL21@P1: implementation to:\nL22@P1: excluded_predefined_functions = [\"search\", \"navigate\"]\nL23@P1: We also deleted all sentences referring to either of these actions from the system instruction. Besides\nL24@P1: these deletions, we made no other benchmark specific modifications or additions to the system\nL25@P1: instruction.\nL26@P1: The evaluations were run using Anchor for the environment and actuation. All sampling parameters\nL27@P1: were left at the default settings in the API reference implementation (temperature=1,\nL28@P1-2: 2include_thoughts=True, etc.). After collecting the trajectories via the API (autoregressive sampling,\nL29@P2: pass@1), we gathered per-trajectory human judgments for the question:\nL30@P2: Did the agent complete the task successfully? Take a close look at the original goal, the steps the AI\nL31@P2: agent took, and the final answer. In your assessment, did the AI agent follow all the instructions,\nL32@P2: complete the task successfully and give a correct answer?\nL33@P2: We gathered three independent human judgments per task, computed success as the majority vote,\nL34@P2: and had the benchmark organizers independently validate the results. The model's final success rate\nL35@P2: was 69.0%, comparing favorably to all prior leaderboard submissions.\nL36@P2: WebVoyager\nL37@P2: The original WebVoyager dataset consisted of 643 task queries. Many of these queries were\nL38@P2: time-sensitive to particular dates in the past (e.g. Show me the list of one-way flights today (February\nL39@P2: 17, 2024) from Chicago to Paris.) or information no longer available (e.g. Check the price for an Apple\nL40@P2: iPhone 14 Plus with 256GB storage in Purple color.). Following industry practice, for the self-reported\nL41@P2: results we used a set for which any explicitly mentioned dates were updated to be in the future, and\nL42@P2: any tasks that were still infeasible were removed, leaving 559 remaining, date-edited tasks. As different\nL43@P2: models of this benchmark are run at different points of time and exclude different tasks, self-reported\nL44@P2: results are difficult to compare. In contrast, the Browserbase results are done over the same query sets\nL45@P2: with the same websites accessed on the same day. Actuation, sampling, settings, and human\nL46@P2: evaluation followed the same process as for Online-Mind2Web above.\nL47@P2: AndroidWorld\nL48@P2: Adaptations for mobile with the Gemini 2.5 Computer Use API\nL49@P2: AndroidWorld requires operating in a mobile rather than web environment. Assessing the Gemini 2.5\nL50@P2: Computer Use API and model on this benchmark required modifying the action space and the SI to fit\nL51@P2: the mobile environment. The below predefined functions were excluded:\n"
